Narrator: [Scene 6B: Branch B - Complete Burnout] [HIDE: all]
Narrator: [BACKGROUND: Jules's dorm room, 11:00 a.m. The blinds are drawn shut, keeping the room in a cold, artificial twilight.]

Narrator: [SPRITE: Jules_Stressed, middle] Jules's phone buzzes on the desk. A text from Maya: *Coffee or pizza later? Nora's in.*
Narrator: Jules stares at the screen. He types a single thumbs-up emoji, hits send, and immediately silences all notifications.
Narrator: He opens the discussion section document for an eighth rewrite that no one requested. He logs back into his terminal and chases the two compiler warnings down to zero. Then he compiles it again, just to be sure.
Jules_Tired(middle): (V.O.) I don't have room for a diner and a trailhead. I have room for the 3.9 and the interview. That's the whole equation. That's always been the whole equation.

Narrator: [BACKGROUND: Jules's dorm room, Friday, 9:00 p.m. The room is pitch black except for the blinding blue glare of the monitors.]
Narrator: [AUDIO: The loud, lonely buzz of a vibrating cell phone against bare wood.]

Narrator: [SPRITE: Jules_Distressed, middle] His father calls. Jules answers on the second ring.
Dad: (delighted, shouting over music) Three point nine, Julian! RTX on Monday! I'm telling everyone at work on Monday!
Jules_Distressed(middle): (voice completely flat, hollow, robotic) Yeah, Pa. Three point nine holds.
Dad: That's my boy. Unstoppable. Get some rest this weekend!
Narrator: The line goes dead. Jules lowers the phone.
Narrator: He doesn't mention that he hasn't eaten a real meal since Tuesday. He doesn't mention Maya's and Nora's unanswered texts, or the shoebox of flashcards now spilling onto the floor.
Narrator: He got everything he set out to get this week. The GPA held. The interview is on the calendar. By every external metric known to his family and the department, he is an unqualified success.
Narrator: He falls asleep sitting upright in his desk chair, his chest vibrating faintly from too much caffeine, his phone clutched face-up in his hand in case RTX emails early.
Narrator: [HIDE: all] The room is quiet. He is entirely, precisely alone. Like he always has been...
System: [FINAL STATS: Burnout CRITICAL | Vulnerability ZERO | Health DEPLETED]
Narrator: "The weight was still there. It was still only his to bear, and his alone. He didn't want to be a burden. Now he knows it..."
